---

<CaseLayout
  title="TLS-Assist"
  description="A modular traffic light and sign recognition layer for LLM-based autonomous driving, published at IEEE IV 2026."
  index="01"
  category="Research + engineering"
  subtitle="Giving an LLM driver explicit traffic-rule perception: how a modular layer for traffic lights and signs improved closed-loop driving performance in CARLA."
  meta={[
    { label: 'Role', value: 'Perception research · co-author' },
    { label: 'Timeline', value: '2025 – 2026' },
    { label: 'Venue', value: 'IEEE IV 2026' },
    { label: 'Stack', value: 'PyTorch · YOLO11 · CARLA · LMDrive · BEVDriver' },
  ]}
  next={next}
>
  <Fragment slot="media">
    <nav class="case-toc" aria-label="On this page">
      <a href="#problem">01 / Problem</a>
      <a href="#approach">02 / Approach</a>
      <a href="#evidence">03 / Evidence</a>
      <a href="#notes">04 / Engineering notes</a>
      <a href="#publication">05 / Publication</a>
    </nav>

    <figure class="case-lead-media">
      <div class="figure__media">
        <img
          src="/images/tlsr-night.webp"
          alt="Night-time CARLA scene with a green traffic light, three camera views, and a natural-language traffic-light notice"
          width="1200"
          height="900"
          fetchpriority="high"
          decoding="async"
        />
      </div>
      <figcaption>
        Closed-loop CARLA evaluation. The perception layer converts a green light into a structured notice — “Green signal, get ready and go ahead” — for the LLM planner.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </Fragment>

  <CaseSection number="01" title="An LLM can reason about the route. It cannot reliably see the light." id="problem">
    <p class="case-lead">
      LLM-based driving agents brought a new kind of reasoning to autonomous driving. They also inherited a very old perception problem.
    </p>
    <p>
      LMDrive and similar systems combine camera input with natural-language instructions. The planner can reason about a route, but small, safety-critical objects — traffic lights and signs — are easy to miss and easy to misread. In CARLA's LangAuto benchmark, that showed up as a disproportionate number of traffic-light and sign infractions.
    </p>
    <p>
      Adding more capacity to the planner does not fix a missing observation. The missing piece was an explicit perception layer that could answer three questions reliably: <strong>Is there a relevant traffic light or sign? What state is it in? How do we tell the planner in language it already understands?</strong>
    </p>
    <div class="case-callout">
      The goal was not a new planner. It was a modular redundancy layer that made traffic rules explicit in the prompt — without changing the driving agent's underlying architecture.
    </div>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection number="02" title="A modular redundancy layer, not a black box." id="approach">
    <p class="case-lead">
      TLS-Assist sits beside the driving agent. Multi-view images go in; structured traffic-rule messages come out.
    </p>

    <div class="case-pipeline" aria-label="TLS-Assist pipeline">
      <div class="pipeline-step">
        <span class="pipeline-step__index">01</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Multi-view input</h3>
          <p>Left, focus, and right camera views from the CARLA ego vehicle, supporting both single-view and multi-view configurations.</p>
          <span class="pipeline-step__meta">CARLA · RGB cameras</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pipeline-step">
        <span class="pipeline-step__index">02</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Traffic light &amp; sign detection</h3>
          <p>YOLO11 detectors pre-trained on real-world data, then fine-tuned inside CARLA. A dedicated traffic-sign dataset was prepared for the sign model.</p>
          <span class="pipeline-step__meta">PyTorch · YOLO11 · transfer learning</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pipeline-step">
        <span class="pipeline-step__index">03</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Relevance prediction</h3>
          <p>Not every light in frame applies to the ego vehicle. The relevance module selects the traffic light that matters for the current lane and approach.</p>
          <span class="pipeline-step__meta">Geometry · ego-vehicle context</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pipeline-step">
        <span class="pipeline-step__index">04</span>
        <div>
          <h3>State validation</h3>
          <p>A temporal buffer and consistency check suppress flicker and reduce the chance of acting on a single misclassified frame.</p>
          <span class="pipeline-step__meta">Temporal reasoning · heuristics</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pipeline-step">
        <span class="pipeline-step__index">05</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Message generator</h3>
          <p>Detections and validated states become structured natural-language notices, injected directly into the LLM prompt.</p>
          <span class="pipeline-step__meta">Structured language · prompt injection</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pipeline-step">
        <span class="pipeline-step__index">06</span>
        <div>
          <h3>LLM planner</h3>
          <p>LMDrive or BEVDriver consumes the notice and produces the next driving action. The layer is plug-and-play and model-agnostic.</p>
          <span class="pipeline-step__meta">LMDrive · BEVDriver · LangAuto</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p>
      The design deliberately keeps the agent unchanged. TLS-Assist is a perception sidecar: it can be dropped into any CARLA-based, language-conditioned driving stack that accepts image input and natural-language instructions. That constraint made the work harder to tune, but much easier to reuse.
    </p>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection number="03" title="The metric that matters is driving behaviour." id="evidence">
    <p class="case-lead">
      Detection accuracy is necessary, but not sufficient. The system was evaluated in closed-loop simulation, where a missed light becomes an infraction and a wrong state becomes a manoeuvre.
    </p>

    <div class="case-metrics">
      <div class="case-metric">
        <strong>+14%</strong>
        <span>relative driving performance vs LMDrive</span>
      </div>
      <div class="case-metric">
        <strong>+7%</strong>
        <span>relative driving performance vs BEVDriver</span>
      </div>
      <div class="case-metric">
        <strong>Fewer</strong>
        <span>traffic-light and sign infractions across routes</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p>
      The published results report relative driving-performance improvements of up to <strong>14% over LMDrive</strong> and <strong>7% over BEVDriver</strong>, alongside a consistent reduction in traffic-light and sign infractions. Those numbers come from the IEEE IV 2026 paper.
    </p>

    <p>
      The master's thesis contains a deeper route-level evaluation. On the LangAuto Long route set, the red-light score dropped from <strong>9.7 to 3.8</strong>, and the infraction score rose from <strong>0.73 to 0.77</strong>. On the Tiny and Short sets, the improvements were smaller — a useful reminder that context and route difficulty matter.
    </p>

    <div class="case-gallery">
      <figure class="case-figure">
        <div class="figure__media">
          <img src="/images/red-light-score.png" alt="Chart comparing red-light scores for LMDrive and TLS-Assist across Tiny, Short, and Long route sets" width="1485" height="874" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        </div>
        <figcaption>Red-light score by route set. Lower is better; the largest reduction appears on the Long set (9.7 → 3.8).</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="case-figure">
        <div class="figure__media">
          <img src="/images/infraction-score.png" alt="Chart comparing infraction scores for LMDrive, TLR, TSR, and TLS-Assist across Tiny, Short, and Long route sets" width="1485" height="863" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        </div>
        <figcaption>Infraction score by route set. Higher is better; TLS-Assist tracks or improves on LMDrive across all three sets.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
    <p class="case-note">
      Thesis results and paper results come from different evaluation runs. They are presented as complementary evidence, not as a single leaderboard.
    </p>

    <div class="case-gallery">
      <figure class="case-figure">
        <div class="figure__media">
          <img src="/images/tlsr-night.webp" alt="Night driving scene with a green traffic light and a natural-language traffic-light notice" width="1200" height="900" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        </div>
        <figcaption>Traffic-light state converted into a natural-language notice for the planner.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="case-figure">
        <div class="figure__media">
          <img src="/images/tlsr-stop.webp" alt="Daytime CARLA scene approaching a stop sign with a traffic-sign notice" width="1200" height="900" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        </div>
        <figcaption>Traffic-sign recognition in a daytime route: “Be mindful of stop sign.”</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <figure class="case-figure">
      <div class="figure__media">
        <img src="/images/tls-assist-yellow.webp" alt="CARLA yellow-light frame with left, focus, and right camera views, and a TrafficLightNotice injected into the driving agent's prompt" width="1200" height="900" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
      </div>
      <figcaption>
        A yellow-light frame from the closed-loop run: three camera views, the validated signal state, and the
        natural-language notice added to the planner's input.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection number="04" title="What was hard — and what I would change." id="notes">
    <p class="case-lead">
      The interesting engineering was not the happy path. It was the failure modes that only appear when perception, language, and simulation meet.
    </p>

    <div class="notes-grid">
      <div class="note-card">
        <h3>Two detectors, one compute budget</h3>
        <p>Separate traffic-light and traffic-sign models are simple to reason about, but expensive next to an LLM and a vision encoder. A shared backbone — or reuse of the agent's vision features — is the obvious next step.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="note-card">
        <h3>Heuristics before learned modules</h3>
        <p>Relevance prediction and state validation were deliberately heuristic baselines. They are interpretable and cheap, but a learned or probabilistic alternative could handle occlusions and unusual junctions better.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="note-card">
        <h3>Simulation is noisy</h3>
        <p>CARLA's dynamics and the LLM's sensitivity to prompt wording introduce run-to-run variance. The evaluation spans multiple route sets for that reason; a single run would overstate the result.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="note-card">
        <h3>Modularity is a feature</h3>
        <p>The layer is model-agnostic and works with single- or multi-view cameras. That reusability is what made integration with both LMDrive and BEVDriver practical.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection number="05" title="Publication, code, and models." id="publication">
    <p class="case-lead">
      The work is public: paper, integration code, and fine-tuned models for both driving agents.
    </p>

    <div class="publication-card">
      <span class="mono accent">IEEE Intelligent Vehicles Symposium 2026</span>
      <h3>Enhancing LLM-based Autonomous Driving with Modular Traffic Light and Sign Recognition</h3>
      <p class="publication-authors">
        Fabian Schmidt · <strong>Noushiq Mohammed Kayilan Abdul Nazar</strong> · Markus Enzweiler · Abhinav Valada
      </p>
      <p class="publication-affiliation">
        Esslingen University of Applied Sciences · University of Freiburg
      </p>
      <p>
        TLS-Assist is a modular redundancy layer for LLM-based driving agents. It detects traffic lights and signs, selects the relevant signal, validates its state over time, and converts the result into structured natural language for the planner.
      </p>
      <div class="case-links">
        <a class="btn btn--primary" href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.14391" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
          arXiv paper
          <svg width="12" height="12" viewBox="0 0 12 12" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
            <path d="M2.5 9.5 9.5 2.5M4 2.5h5.5V8" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" />
          </svg>
        </a>
        <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="https://github.com/iis-esslingen/TLS-Assist" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Code</a>
        <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="https://huggingface.co/iis-esslingen/TLS-Assist" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Models</a>
        <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/research/">Research page</a>
      </div>
      <p class="case-note">Code and models are released by the Institute for Intelligent Systems (IIS).</p>
    </div>
  </CaseSection>
</CaseLayout>

